<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
<head>
  <title>CanviPrj: Change Geographical Reference System (cartographic
  projection, datum, etc) of raster and vector layers</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<h1><span id="wpstitol"/></h1>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

  <h3>Introduction</h3>Cartographic information attempts to express measurable
  characteristics or parameters in our three-dimensional world on paper maps
  that are plane. To achieve this, projection systems are used. In cartography
  a <b>projection system</b> is a coordinate transformation from the Earth
  three-dimensional system to a bidimensional surface. The most important
  properties a projection system should preserve throughout the map are:
  scale, shape (conformal) and area (equivalent or equal-area). However, it is
  impossible to define a projection system having the three properties at the
  same time and, therefore, the optimal projection depends on the region that
  is going to be mapped and on the application that the map will have. For
  example, it is recommended to use UTM projection for local precise
  applications, and Goode Homolosine projection for global scale maps. Lambert
  Conformal Conic is suitable for navigation (direction) applications while
  Lambert Azimuthal Equivalent is more proper for thematic applications.

  <p>A <b>reference system</b> is defined by a set of parameters that have to
  be specified in the chosen projection system. For example, in the UTM
  projection, the zone and the hemisphere must be specified. This way, in
  Catalonia we use the reference system UTM-31N, while in Portugal UTM-29N is
  used and in Lesotho UTM-35S is used.</p>

  <p>It is very useful to have all the maps that we use in the same projection
  and reference system, so that it is easy to combine information in the
  Geographical Information System (GIS). In fact, most of digital GIS allow to
  operate with different information layers only if they are in the same
  reference system and projection. In digital systems it is possible to
  develop a routine to convert a map from one projection and reference system
  to another, such it is CanviPrj of MiraMon.</p>
  <hr/>

  <h3>CanviPrj</h3>This program allows to transform from one projection system
  to another and allows, by setting the projection parameters, to change the
  reference system as well. It works on raster files (IMG), non structured
  vector files (VEC) and structured vector files of points (PNT), arcs (ARC)
  and polygons (POL). To transform node (NOD) files, it is required to
  transform their associated arc files, and the corresponding NOD will be
  automatically transformed.
  <!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

  <p>If the origin map has no projection system because it has been never
  georeferenced, you should first use the program <a href="CORRGEOM.HTM">CORRGEOM</a>.</p>
  <hr/>

  <exclusiva>
<!-- Aquí inicia la part corresponent a la sintaxi de miramon. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpssintaxi"/>
<!-- Aquí acaba la part corresponent a la sintaxi de miramon editable de el WPS.-->
    </ul>Examples:
    <pre>
CANVIPRJ MUNI_LL.VEC  MUNI_UTM UTM-31N  1
CANVIPRJ CATA_UTM.IMG CATA2_LL lat/long 1 450000 480500 4724500 4750000 30 0
CANVIPRJ CATA_UTM.IMG CATA3       x     x    x      x      x        x    x x
CANVIPRJ MA89_UTM.ARC MA89_LCC Lambert_Conformal_Conic
</pre><!-- $$EXCLUSIVA$$

To obtain the complete help, please type:
CANVIPRJ ?
-->
  </exclusiva>
  <hr/>
  <!--
  <UL>
     <LI>Azimuthal_Equidistant: Azimuthal Equidistant (spherical model).
     <LI>Cylindrical_Equal_Area: Cylindrical Equal-Area (spherical model).
     <LI>Goode_Homolosine: Goode Homolosine (spherical model).
     <LI>Lambert_Azimuthal_Equal_Area: Lambert Azimuthal Equal-Area (spherical model).
     <LI>Lambert_Conformal_Conic: Lambert Conformal Conic.
     <LI>lat/long: Equirectangular (Latitude/Longitude in degrees).
     <LI>Mercator: Mercator (ellipsoidal model).
     <LI>Sinusoidal: Sinusoidal (spherical model).
     <LI>Transverse_Mercator: Transverse Mercator. Gauss (ellipsoidal model) 
     <LI>UTM-$$?: UTM (Universal Transverse Mercator) where $$ indicates the zone number 
         (from 1 to 60) and ? designs the hemisphere (N or S) (ellipsoidal model).

Projection parameters can be controlled by the user by changing its values in the file 
CANVIPRJ.INI. This file can be in the working directory or in the program directory. 
If it exists in both places, the working directory has priority. In file CANVIPRJ.INI 
all input (in sections [I_???]) and output (in sections [O_???]) projection parameters 
are described. If file CANVIPRJ.INI does not exist, the program will take the default 
values indicated below. In the UTM projection system, zone and hemisphere are obtained 
from the documentation file (input) and from the command line or interactively (output).<P>

Example of CANVIPRJ.INI file format:
<PRE>
;========= Azimuthal Equidistant (spherical model) =============
[I_Azimuthal_Equidistant]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
fi_1=90.0

[O_Azimuthal_Equidistant]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
fi_1=90.0

;========= Cylindrical Equal-Area (spherical model) ===========
[I_Cylindrical_Equal_Area]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=15.0
fi_S=0.0

[O_Cylindrical_Equal_Area]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=15.0
fi_S=0.0

;====== Goode Holosine (spherical model) =========
[I_Goode_Homolosine]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=-100.0
lambda_1=-100.0
lambda_2=30.0
lambda_3=30.0
lambda_4=-160.0
lambda_5=-60.0
lambda_6=-160.0
lambda_7=-60.0
lambda_8=20.0
lambda_9=140.0
lambda_10=20.0
lambda_11=140.0

[O_Goode_Homolosine]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=-100.0
lambda_1=-100.0
lambda_2=30.0
lambda_3=30.0
lambda_4=-160.0
lambda_5=-60.0
lambda_6=-160.0
lambda_7=-60.0
lambda_8=20.0
lambda_9=140.0
lambda_10=20.0
lambda_11=140.0

;========= Lambert Azimuthal Equal-Area (spherical model) =============
[I_Lambert_Azimuthal_Equal_Area]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
fi_1=90.0

[O_Lambert_Azimuthal_Equal_Area]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
fi_1=90.0

;======== Lambert Conformal Conic (spherical model) ==========
[I_Lambert_Conformal_Conic]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
fi_0=0.0
fi_1=33.0
fi_2=45.0

[O_Lambert_Conformal_Conic]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
fi_0=0.0
fi_1=33.0
fi_2=45.0

;============= Latitude/Longitude in degrees ===================
;No parameters

;============= Mercator ====================
[I_Mercator]
;WGS 84
Semi_major_axis=6378137.0
Reciprocal_flattening=298.257223563
lambda_0=0.0

[O_Mercator]
;WGS 84
Semi_major_axis=6378137.0
Reciprocal_flattening=298.257223563
lambda_0=0.0

;============= Sinusoidal (spherical model) ===================
[I_Sinusoidal]
;Clark spheroid, 1886
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0

[O_Sinusoidal]
radi_terra1=6370997.0
lambda_0=0.0
;============= Transversal de Mercator ====================
[I_Transverse_Mercator]
;Esferoide Internacional
radi_terra1=6378388.0
radi_terra2=6356911.9
c_tissot=0.9996
lambda_0=0.0

[O_Transverse_Mercator]
radi_terra1=6378388.0
radi_terra2=6356911.9
c_tissot=0.9996
lambda_0=0.0
;================= UTM (Universal Transverse Mercator) ====================
;Zone and hemisphere are indicated in the documentation file
[I_UTM]
radi_terra1=6378388.0
radi_terra2=6356912.0
c_tissot=0.9996

;Zone and hemisphere can be indicated at command line or interactively

[O_UTM]
radi_terra1=6378388.0
radi_terra2=6356912.0
c_tissot=0.9996
</PRE>
-->
</body>
